    "engine": "julia",
    "markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Finite-state projection\"\nsubtitle: \"Appendix A05 · solving the master equation directly\"\ncourse-unit: a05-finite-state-projection\nstatus: complete\n---\n\n## Why this unit matters\n\n- Stochastic SIR is usually studied by **simulating trajectories** (the jump processes chapter).\n- *Finite-state projection* (FSP) takes the opposite route: solve directly for\n  the **probability distribution** over states by integrating a truncated\n  chemical master equation [@munsky2006].\n- One deterministic ODE solve returns the *entire* distribution — including tail\n  probabilities that ensembles estimate only with huge sample sizes.\n\n::: {.notes}\nFrame FSP as a change of solution method, not of model. The same CTMC that\nGillespie samples is here integrated as a linear ODE over probabilities. The\npunchline of the deck is the strength/weakness trade-off: exact distributions\nversus state-space explosion.\n:::\n\n## Model and the projected state space\n\nTwo reaction channels on integer counts $(S,I)$, with $R = N-S-I$:\n\n$$\nS + I \\xrightarrow{\\ \\beta\\ } 2I, \\qquad I \\xrightarrow{\\ \\gamma\\ } \\varnothing .\n$$\n\n- **Stochastic** jump process, **continuous** time, **discrete** state.\n- Reachable states form a **triangle** $\\{(s,i): s+i \\le N\\}$: infection maps\n  $(s,i)\\mapsto(s{-}1,i{+}1)$, recovery maps $(s,i)\\mapsto(s,i{-}1)$.\n- We use $N = 100$, $S(0)=99$, $I(0)=1$; $\\beta = \\beta_{\\text{base}}c/N = 0.005$,\n  $\\gamma = 0.25$. $N=100$ is deliberate — the triangle has $(N{+}1)(N{+}2)/2$\n  states.\n\n::: {.notes}\nExplain why the reachable set is a triangle, not a rectangle: $s+i$ can only\nstay constant (infection) or the process only removes infectious individuals\n(recovery), so from $(99,1)$ you never exceed $s+i=100$. This is what our kernel\nexploits.\n:::\n\n## The chemical master equation as a linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{dp(s,i,t)}{dt} =\n\\beta(s{+}1)(i{-}1)\\,p(s{+}1,i{-}1)\n+ \\gamma(i{+}1)\\,p(s,i{+}1)\n- [\\beta s i + \\gamma i]\\,p(s,i).\n$$\n\nStacking probabilities gives $\\dfrac{d\\mathbf{p}}{dt} = \\mathbf{Q}\\,\\mathbf{p}$,\nwith $\\mathbf{Q}$ the **sparse CTMC generator**.\n\n> **We do not use `FiniteStateProjection.jl`.** Its resolved release (v0.3.5)\n> omits `ModelingToolkit` from its dependencies yet calls it internally, so the\n> only way to force it is to mutate a third-party module at runtime. Instead we\n> build $\\mathbf{Q}$ **transparently** — the method and results are identical.\n\n::: {.notes}\nThe compatibility gap is real: working around it means reaching into a package's\nprivate namespace at runtime, which is not something to teach. Building the\ngenerator by hand takes a few lines, stays inside the standard SciML stack, and\nshows what the package would have assembled anyway.\n:::\n\n## Assembling the sparse generator\n\n::: {#cell-a05-slide-generator .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.julia .cell-code}\nusing OrdinaryDiffEq, SparseArrays, LinearAlgebra, StableRNGs, CairoMakie\n\nfunction sir_fsp_generator(N, β, γ; Smax = N, Imax = N)\n    index = Dict{Tuple{Int,Int},Int}(); states = Tuple{Int,Int}[]\n    for s in 0:Smax, i in 0:Imax\n        if s + i <= N\n            push!(states, (s, i)); index[(s, i)] = length(states)\n        end\n    end\n    rows = Int[]; cols = Int[]; vals = Float64[]\n    add!(r, c, v) = (push!(rows, r); push!(cols, c); push!(vals, v))\n    for (col, (s, i)) in enumerate(states)\n        a_inf = β*s*i; a_rec = γ*i\n        add!(col, col, -(a_inf + a_rec))\n        a_inf > 0 && haskey(index, (s-1, i+1)) && add!(index[(s-1, i+1)], col, a_inf)\n        a_rec > 0 && haskey(index, (s, i-1))   && add!(index[(s, i-1)],   col, a_rec)\n    end\n    return sparse(rows, cols, vals, length(states), length(states)), states, index\nend\n\nNcap = 100; β = 0.005; γ = 0.25\nQ, states, index = sir_fsp_generator(Ncap, β, γ)\n(states = length(states), nonzeros = nnz(Q))\n```\n\n::: {#a05-slide-generator .cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n(states = 5151, nonzeros = 15151)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n\n- **5151 states**, a sparse generator with only **15151 nonzeros**.\n\n::: {.notes}\nPoint out that the generator is assembled once and reused across the book,\nthese slides, and the notebook. Sparsity is what makes the linear ODE cheap.\n:::\n\n## Solve, marginalise, validate\n\n::: {#cell-fig-a05-slide-marginal .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.julia .cell-code}\np0 = zeros(length(states)); p0[index[(99, 1)]] = 1.0\nsol = solve(ODEProblem((dp, p, Q, t) -> mul!(dp, Q, p), p0, (0.0, 40.0), Q),\n    Vern7(); abstol = 1e-10, reltol = 1e-8, saveat = 1.0)\n\nmarginalI(p) = (m = zeros(Ncap + 1); for (k, (s, i)) in enumerate(states); m[i+1] += p[k]; end; m)\nPI20 = marginalI(sol.u[21]); counts = 0:Ncap\nmeanI = sum(counts .* PI20)\nleak = 1 - minimum(sum(u) for u in sol.u)\n\nfig = Figure(size = (900, 380))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1]; xlabel = \"infectious count I at t = 20\", ylabel = \"probability\",\n    title = \"FSP mean I(20) ≈ $(round(meanI; digits = 2)),  leakage ≈ $(round(leak; sigdigits = 1))\")\nbarplot!(ax, counts, PI20; color = (:firebrick, 0.7))\nvlines!(ax, [meanI]; color = :black, linestyle = :dash)\nxlims!(ax, -1, 40)\nfig\n```\n\n::: {#fig-a05-slide-marginal .cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```{=html}\n<img width=900 height=380 style='object-fit: contain; height: auto;' src=\"data:image/png;base64, \"/>\n```\n\nFSP marginal for the infectious count at t = 20 (bars) matches a histogram of 2000 seeded Gillespie trajectories (outline); both share essentially the same mean (dashed).\n:::\n:::\n\n\n\n- **Probability conservation**: the closed triangle leaks only at solver\n  tolerance ($\\sim 10^{-16}$). **Mean** matches a Gillespie ensemble.\n\n::: {.notes}\nThe conservation check is the built-in diagnostic for truncation error: on the\nfull triangle nothing escapes. Exercise 1 deliberately truncates $I_{\\max}$ to\nmake probability leak and studies when. The book adds the explicit\nGillespie-ensemble overlay; here we report the matching mean to keep the deck\nlight.\n:::\n\n## Cost: the defining limitation\n\n::: {#a05-slide-growth .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.julia .cell-code}\nusing Printf\nfor n in (50, 100, 250, 1000)\n    tri = ((n + 1) * (n + 2)) ÷ 2\n    @printf(\"N = %5d  →  triangular CME dimension = %8d  (rectangular %d)\\n\",\n        n, tri, (n + 1)^2)\nend\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}\n```\nN =    50  →  triangular CME dimension =     1326  (rectangular 2601)\nN =   100  →  triangular CME dimension =     5151  (rectangular 10201)\nN =   250  →  triangular CME dimension =    31626  (rectangular 63001)\nN =  1000  →  triangular CME dimension =   501501  (rectangular 1002001)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n\n- Doubling $N$ roughly **quadruples** the equations; a third species makes it\n  cubic — why we used $N=100$, not the baseline $N=1000$ ($\\sim 5\\times10^5$\n  states).\n- **Truncation is an approximation**; the conservation check is the diagnostic.\n- **Best for small populations / few species**: early-outbreak, extinction, and\n  tail questions. For large $N$, use ensembles (Ch. 4) or diffusion\n  approximations (Ch. 5).\n\nSee the [book chapter](../../appendices/05-finite-state-projection.html) and the\n[Catalyst](https://docs.sciml.ai/Catalyst/stable/) /\n[ModelingToolkit](https://docs.sciml.ai/ModelingToolkit/stable/) docs.\n\n::: {.notes}\nClose on the trade-off: FSP is exact and sampling-free but dimensionality-bound.\nReachability (the triangle) only halves the constant factor, not the order of\ngrowth — that is Exercise 2. The notebook lets the audience slide the truncation\nand watch leakage appear.\n:::\n\n",
